#### 3.1 HOD: Department Administrator

HOD has administrative authority over their explicitly authorized department.

The HOD can:

- Manage department students, faculty, mentors and permitted user accounts.
- Create invitations and activate, deactivate, suspend or reactivate accounts with security safeguards.
- Manage department teams, memberships and project records.
- Review proposals and approve, reject or request corrections.
- Allocate mentors and monitor mentor workload.
- Assign external evaluators to eligible department projects.
- Manage milestones, tasks, review schedules and deadlines.
- Configure authorized department academic settings within applicable policies.
- Configure evaluation rubrics and weights within permitted limits.
- Monitor submissions, reviews, corrections and evaluations.
- Moderate evaluations when the configured workflow allows.
- Authorize and publish results.
- Manage department announcements, notifications and calendars.
- View department analytics and generate PDF, Excel and authorized CSV reports.
- Review department audit history.
- Manage academic-year rollover, project closure and archival.
- Review authorized backup status, integration health and operational information through a safe, read-only interface.

HOD must not need a separate administrator account.

HOD must not access database credentials, application secrets, unrestricted infrastructure controls or another department's private records.

#### 3.2 Student

Students can:

- View and manage their authorized team memberships.
- Create, revise and submit project proposals.
- View proposal status and approval feedback.
- Access their assigned project information.
- Manage permitted tasks, milestones and progress updates.
- Upload documents, reports, presentations and evidence.
- View submission and document revision history.
- Participate in project discussions and collaboration.
- Track feedback and submit corrections.
- View review schedules, deadlines and notifications.
- View evaluation results only when publication and authorization rules permit.
- Export permitted project portfolios and summaries.

Students must not approve their own proposals, assign themselves official mentors or evaluators, change official marks or publish results.

#### 3.3 Project Mentor

Project mentors can:

- View projects explicitly assigned to them.
- Monitor assigned teams and project progress.
- Review proposals and submissions where authorized.
- Create and update feedback, tasks and milestone reviews.
- Schedule or participate in project reviews according to permissions.
- Request corrections and verify corrected submissions.
- Record progress observations and meeting outcomes.
- Monitor overdue tasks and milestones.
- View authorized project documents and activity history.

Mentors must not access unassigned projects or modify finalized evaluations and published results outside an explicitly authorized workflow.

#### 3.4 External Evaluator

External evaluators can:

- View projects explicitly assigned to them for evaluation.
- Access only the submissions and documents permitted for those assignments.
- Review evaluation rubrics and criteria.
- Save evaluation drafts.
- Enter scores and comments within configured limits.
- Provide required comments for designated outcomes.
- Submit and finalize evaluations according to workflow rules.
- Declare conflicts of interest.
- View their own evaluation status and history.

External evaluators must not browse unrelated student records, access unassigned projects, change published results or edit finalized evaluations without a specific authorized correction workflow.

### 4. AUTHORIZATION AND SECURITY BOUNDARIES

Enforce permissions on the backend for every protected operation.

- Never trust client-supplied roles or permission claims.
- Never trust client-supplied department IDs, ownership, project assignments, evaluator IDs or approval states.
- Resolve identity from the authenticated server-side session.
- Scope HOD operations to the HOD's authorized department.
- Scope mentor operations to explicitly assigned projects.
- Scope student operations to valid memberships and permitted resources.
- Scope evaluator operations to explicit assignments and allowed evaluation states.
- Enforce object-level authorization on reads, updates, deletes, uploads, downloads and exports.
- Prevent IDOR, cross-department access and privilege escalation.
- Record sensitive administrative actions in audit logs.
- Reject invalid workflow transitions on the server.
- Use transactions for related multi-step operations.
- Apply least privilege to every route and service.

Create a complete `PERMISSIONS_MATRIX.md` mapping all four roles to every major operation.

Test both allowed and denied access. Hiding a button in the UI is never a substitute for backend authorization.

